From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rvwasm <rvwasm@localhost>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 23:41:48 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] wasm: end a task that runs its program when a fatal
 signal comes

A task's program runs in its Worker until it makes a system call, and only
there does the kernel act on signals; nothing can interrupt Wasm code.
SIGKILL did not end a program in a loop; an exit_group() of a process with
a thread that computes, or an exec() from such a process, waited for that
thread for good (zap_other_threads(), de_thread()), and the parent could not
reap the zombie leader.

The host can end the Worker, and that is safe while the Worker runs the
program and never once it has entered the kernel. thread_info gets a
user_state word: the task sets it to RUNNING last thing on its way back to
the program and then looks for a fatal signal once more; on every entry
(system calls, wasm_user_fault) it takes RUNNING or KILL back to IN_KERNEL
with compare-and-swap. A fatal signal reaches the task's CPU as the
reschedule IPI of kick_process(); arch_smp_send_reschedule() now looks at
that CPU's current task (wasm_cpu_curr(), rq->curr) and, if it is a user
task with a fatal signal pending whose state is RUNNING, sets KILL and calls
the host's new import wasm_end_user_task(task, &user_state). The host sets
KILL to ENDING (or does nothing: the task entered the kernel meanwhile and
takes the signal there), terminates the Worker and runs the task in a new
one through the new export wasm_user_task_end(task), which becomes the task
as ret_from_fork does and takes the pending fatal signal like any return to
user mode: get_signal(), do_group_exit(). A Worker that enters the kernel
after the host has set ENDING waits there, holding nothing, to be ended.

Non-fatal signals still wait for the program's next system call; a program
that wants them sooner has to make one now and then.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
